WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.649 --> 00:00:07.830
Stan Lee is one of the most recognizable figures
in pop culture.

00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:12.219
Not only did he co-create of many of the world's
most popular superheroes, he frequently appears

00:00:12.219 --> 00:00:15.869
alongside them in movies, TV shows, and comic
books.

00:00:15.869 --> 00:00:19.100
Here are some our favorite times that Stan
was the man.

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:20.810
Stan the supervillain

00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:24.509
Stan and collaborator Jack Kirby have been
super-involved with their creations since

00:00:24.509 --> 00:00:28.039
the 1950s, when they used to cosplay for their
own comic book covers.

00:00:28.039 --> 00:00:32.550
But they outdid themselves with Fantastic
Four #10, which stars the pair being forced

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:35.640
to assist Dr. Doom in a scheme to defeat Reed
Richards.

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:38.820
Luckily, Reed saves the day in the end.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:40.590
Stan the superhero

00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:45.370
In 1978, Stan went from fighting the Fantastic
Four to becoming team leader.

00:00:45.370 --> 00:00:46.370
What If?

00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:49.990
#11 explores what might've happened if Stan
and the rest of the original Marvel creative

00:00:49.990 --> 00:00:55.340
team—Jack Kirby, Sol Brodsky, and Flo Steinberg—had
become the Fantastic Four.

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:59.070
Spoiler alert: not only do they defeat the
evil aliens, they do it while putting out

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:01.640
comics about their own adventures.

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:04.460
Leave it to Stan to save the world in his
spare time...

00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:06.939
Stan the superhero: part two

00:01:06.939 --> 00:01:10.470
One of the coolest Stan Lee cameos came in
Disney's Big Hero 6.

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:14.950
Based loosely on a Marvel comic that Stan
didn't have anything to do with, the film

00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:18.830
paid tribute to Marvel's living mascot with
a post-credits scene revealing he's actually

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:21.700
the father of superhero wannabe Fred.

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:22.700
Even better?

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:25.560
Stan is secretly a superhero himself.

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:27.060
Stan the librarian

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:30.910
Maybe the funniest of all of Lee's many movie
cameos was in The Amazing Spider-Man.

00:01:30.910 --> 00:01:36.740
Spidey and the Lizard trash an entire library,
but neither of them can lay a finger on Stan,

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:40.880
who's oblivious to their tussle thanks to
his sick Beats By Dre.

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:42.780
Stan the wedding crasher

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:47.100
Considering Stan Lee co-created the Fantastic
Four, you’d think he’d get an invite to

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:48.460
Reed and Sue’s wedding.

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:52.780
But Stan actually had to crash the wedding
not once, but twice: first in the comics,

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:57.170
accompanied by Jack Kirby, and then in the
movies in Fantastic Four: Rise of the Silver

00:01:57.170 --> 00:01:58.170
Surfer.

00:01:58.170 --> 00:02:00.760
He was shown the door both times.

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:02.369
“Ah, invitation sir.

00:02:02.369 --> 00:02:05.470
Um, I should be on that list.

00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:06.470
Name.

00:02:06.470 --> 00:02:07.470
Stan Lee.

00:02:07.470 --> 00:02:08.810
Yeah uh, nice try buddy, nice try.

00:02:08.810 --> 00:02:09.800
No really...?”

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:12.719
Stan the guru

00:02:12.719 --> 00:02:17.440
Throughout Kevin Smith's 1995 cul- classic
Mallrats, Jason Lee’s character Brodie talks

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:20.719
about Stan Lee's awesomeness—just like we’re
doing now.

00:02:20.719 --> 00:02:25.150
At a key moment, Stan suddenly shows up and
delivers a rousing inspirational speech that

00:02:25.150 --> 00:02:28.680
turns Brodie around, allowing him to save
the day.

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:31.420
If only it were that easy to summon Stan when
you needed him.

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:35.359
We’ll just have to settle for his doll instead.

00:02:35.359 --> 00:02:36.599
Stan the Man

00:02:36.599 --> 00:02:40.340
Given that he's pushing a hundred, Stan is
like everyone's grandpa.

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:43.599
That's why it was so surprising to see him
show up in a strip club in Deadpool.

00:02:43.599 --> 00:02:47.019
“Give it up for Chastity.”

00:02:47.019 --> 00:02:50.969
In real life, Stan is anything but a player:
he and his wife Joan have been married for

00:02:50.969 --> 00:02:52.010
seven decades.

00:02:52.010 --> 00:02:56.560
But Stan's strip club appearance is just one
of several cameos showing Stan surrounded

00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:57.609
by ladies.

00:02:57.609 --> 00:03:03.230
In Iron Man,Tony Stark accidentally confuses
Stan for Playboy creator Hugh Hefner, while

00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:06.359
in Guardians of the Galaxy, we see Stan working
his mojo.

00:03:06.359 --> 00:03:11.379
Guardians director James Gunn originally had
a different idea for Stan's cameo, though:

00:03:11.379 --> 00:03:15.730
he wanted Stan to be shown in one of the Collector's
cages, only to have his head blown off when

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:18.989
the Collector's museum is leveled by an explosion.

00:03:18.989 --> 00:03:20.939
Now that’s going out…

00:03:20.939 --> 00:03:23.159
“Like a boss, approve memo, like a boss.”

00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:24.700
Thanks for watching!

00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:28.400
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:31.909
And leave a comment to let us know which Stan
Lee moment you think was his most

00:03:31.909 --> 00:03:32.409
epic...

